WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.800
So welcome everyone to this lecture on robot localization.

00:04.800 --> 00:08.120
So the goal is to provide you with an overview about

00:08.120 --> 00:12.360
different techniques that can be used for robot localization,

00:12.360 --> 00:14.200
and then in the later chapters,

00:14.200 --> 00:16.400
dive deeper into the individual techniques.

00:16.400 --> 00:21.120
So today, I'm only going to provide rough overview what means localization,

00:21.120 --> 00:24.000
what it is about, and what different types of

00:24.000 --> 00:27.400
techniques can be applied in order to localize a robot,

00:27.400 --> 00:29.120
an autonomous car or

00:29.120 --> 00:32.560
another moving vehicle that navigates through the environment.

00:32.560 --> 00:35.360
So there's also a five-minute summary of

00:35.360 --> 00:40.480
this lecture that you may want to watch beforehand as a preparation for this lecture,

00:40.480 --> 00:45.160
and now we want to directly dive into the topic of localization.

00:45.160 --> 00:49.160
So localization basically means where am I?

00:49.160 --> 00:51.160
So we need to answer the question,

00:51.160 --> 00:53.600
where is the robot in the world?

00:53.600 --> 00:57.200
Where is the vehicle driving on a road?

00:57.200 --> 00:58.320
Where are we?

00:58.320 --> 01:02.600
So location information is the central part in here,

01:02.600 --> 01:06.720
and location information or position information matters

01:06.720 --> 01:11.800
because this is the basis in order to navigate effectively through an environment.

01:11.800 --> 01:14.160
So we need to know our pose, for example,

01:14.160 --> 01:16.680
in order to avoid bumping into obstacles,

01:16.680 --> 01:20.240
in order to plan a correct feasible

01:20.240 --> 01:25.360
and efficient to navigate paths from our current location to a goal location.

01:25.440 --> 01:29.600
And so position information is key to a lot of basic robotics tasks,

01:29.600 --> 01:31.400
especially robotic navigation tasks,

01:31.400 --> 01:34.720
and therefore we care about where we are.

01:34.720 --> 01:37.320
And localization, try to answer the question,

01:37.320 --> 01:41.320
where am I, and often also where am I looking to or pointing to,

01:41.320 --> 01:44.000
so in which orientation I'm actually in,

01:44.000 --> 01:49.520
in order to provide relevant information for other downstream tasks.

01:49.520 --> 01:52.720
So the questions where am I means we want to estimate

01:52.720 --> 01:56.480
our position and our orientation or sometimes called heading

01:56.480 --> 02:01.160
of a mobile system in some external or a given reference frame.

02:01.160 --> 02:03.960
So that typically means there's a reference frame given,

02:03.960 --> 02:05.760
could be a GPS reference frame,

02:05.760 --> 02:08.840
but could also be a local reference frame for a building,

02:08.840 --> 02:12.040
for a map, whatever we are using,

02:12.040 --> 02:15.080
and typically localized with respect to a map.

02:15.080 --> 02:17.680
And I want to do this based on sensor information,

02:17.680 --> 02:21.880
assuming I have a mobile system that moves through the environment.

02:21.920 --> 02:24.360
So I want to estimate where is a platform,

02:24.360 --> 02:28.000
so for example an XY or XYZ location as well as a heading,

02:28.000 --> 02:33.080
so where the platform actually looking to in some reference frame.

02:33.080 --> 02:36.920
And for that we typically assume to have information about

02:36.920 --> 02:39.760
what commands is the platform executing.

02:39.760 --> 02:41.800
So if you think about an autonomous car,

02:41.800 --> 02:43.640
it may be what is the steering angle

02:43.640 --> 02:46.800
and what is the whole heart of the gas pedal being pressed

02:46.800 --> 02:51.600
in order to estimate where the platform is going just based on the commands.

02:51.600 --> 02:54.960
Or it could be the odometry commands of a mobile platform.

02:54.960 --> 02:57.480
In addition to that, we typically assume to have sensor data.

02:57.480 --> 03:01.280
The sensor data could be a camera, this could be a laser rangefinder,

03:01.280 --> 03:04.640
this may be a GPS, this might be an IMU sensor.

03:04.640 --> 03:07.480
So sensor data that we are receiving that we want to use

03:07.480 --> 03:11.000
in order to localize ourselves in the environment.

03:11.000 --> 03:15.360
Some of those sensors allow us to localize us with respect to a map

03:15.360 --> 03:17.920
and others with respect to some external reference frame

03:17.920 --> 03:21.560
even without having explicitly a map given.

03:21.760 --> 03:26.760
But quite often in localization we refer to our location

03:26.760 --> 03:29.160
with respect to a map that defines a reference frame

03:29.160 --> 03:32.520
or some other global frame such as the GPS coordinates.

03:32.520 --> 03:36.120
And localization means to answer the question, where am I?

03:36.120 --> 03:38.240
You also know this question as a human,

03:38.240 --> 03:40.040
so if you think about your mobile phone

03:40.040 --> 03:46.040
and you use your favorite map application and press localize me or where am I,

03:46.040 --> 03:49.960
you will have a map and it shows you a spot for example here in our building

03:49.960 --> 03:51.640
which tells you where you are.

03:51.640 --> 03:56.000
And here in this example now you have for example a longitude latitude value given

03:56.000 --> 04:01.560
which describes a position in the GPS coordinate system

04:01.560 --> 04:04.280
so that you can use this kind of global reference frame

04:04.280 --> 04:06.280
in order to describe your locations.

04:06.280 --> 04:08.200
But that's not the only way how we can do this,

04:08.200 --> 04:12.400
we can also use maps and localize us with respect to maps.

04:12.400 --> 04:15.160
So what you see here is actually an old robot,

04:15.160 --> 04:19.400
the robot Rhino developed here in Bonn maybe 25 years ago,

04:19.400 --> 04:23.520
maybe even a bit more and in this example it used a map of the environment,

04:23.520 --> 04:26.040
this was basically a local map where you could see the free space

04:26.040 --> 04:31.440
and the obstacles around and was using its sensors such as sonars or laser range finders

04:31.440 --> 04:35.320
in order to estimate its own pose with respect to those obstacles.

04:35.320 --> 04:38.920
Basically using a sensor model comparing what the platform sees

04:38.920 --> 04:43.000
and how that fits into the map that it has about the environment.

04:43.000 --> 04:49.000
And as a result of this, this platform will estimate its pose within this map

04:49.000 --> 04:53.720
and in this case the map is providing us with our reference frame.

04:53.720 --> 04:57.240
We can also think about autonomous cars navigating through the environment

04:57.240 --> 05:02.040
where you have a vehicle like this equipped with different types of sensors such as cameras,

05:02.040 --> 05:07.920
such as LIDARs, RADARs, GPS IMUs, wheel odometers and other sensors

05:07.920 --> 05:10.640
trying to estimate where it is in the environment.

05:10.640 --> 05:15.240
And here typically we have a map information that could consist of lane markings, of poles,

05:15.240 --> 05:20.400
of buildings or other information about what is where in the environment

05:20.400 --> 05:23.480
that we use as a map in order to localize.

05:23.480 --> 05:28.560
So this describes the problem of robot localization or vehicle localization.

05:28.560 --> 05:34.200
So using our sensor information and using the control commands that the platform is actually receiving,

05:34.200 --> 05:40.080
we want to estimate where are we with respect to some map or other reference frame.

05:40.080 --> 05:45.720
Most of the techniques for robot localization which are used today are probabilistic approaches.

05:45.720 --> 05:51.040
That means they take into account that the observation that we have are not perfect,

05:51.040 --> 05:55.320
that they are affected by noise and the same holds for all motion commands.

05:55.320 --> 06:02.400
So sending a certain motion command, we cannot rely on the fact that the platform precisely executes this motion command.

06:02.400 --> 06:09.240
There will be always noise associated to that because it will never precisely execute the command.

06:09.240 --> 06:13.440
And a probability theory allows us to represent these uncertainties

06:13.440 --> 06:18.400
and take these uncertainties into account when we're performing our estimation approach,

06:18.400 --> 06:21.480
trying to estimate where the platform is and where it's looking to.

06:21.480 --> 06:25.920
So if you think about the non-probabilistic world, it would be for the vehicle, for example, the description.

06:25.920 --> 06:31.080
The vehicle is exactly here if we describe this here kind of as a 1D space

06:31.080 --> 06:35.600
where X is the 1D variable describing where the platform is.

06:35.600 --> 06:40.640
Then the standard or non-probabilistic localization would say you're precisely here.

06:40.640 --> 06:44.480
And the probabilistic approach may fit a distribution which may look like a Gaussian

06:44.480 --> 06:49.360
but could also be any other type of distribution saying the vehicle is somewhere over here

06:49.360 --> 06:55.720
and the higher the value, the higher the probability that the platform is in a certain region of the environment.

06:55.720 --> 06:59.520
And we are typically looking into estimating such a probability distribution

06:59.520 --> 07:03.280
in order to describe where the platform is in the environment.

07:03.280 --> 07:07.600
So we are performing state estimation or probabilistic state estimation.

07:07.600 --> 07:10.120
So what's the state? Which state are we estimating?

07:10.120 --> 07:15.320
So in localization, we are often needing to estimate the position of the platform and its heading.

07:15.320 --> 07:17.040
So where is it looking to?

07:17.040 --> 07:20.560
And if you're living in a 2D world, in this case,

07:20.560 --> 07:26.920
then we typically have a three-dimensional vector describing an XY location and the heading as a 1D heading.

07:26.920 --> 07:30.720
Where is the platform looking into? In which direction is it looking into?

07:30.720 --> 07:33.760
So we have three variables that we need to estimate.

07:33.760 --> 07:38.360
If we think about a 3D localization with six degrees, then we have typically six degrees of freedom.

07:38.360 --> 07:43.240
So we have an XY and V coordinate and then three rotational angles, for example,

07:43.240 --> 07:49.920
a roll, pitch, yaw describing the three different angles the platform can be looking to.

07:49.920 --> 07:56.320
So we either have to estimate a 3D or a 6D vector in order to estimate the current position of the platform.

07:56.320 --> 07:59.280
And often we're actually interested in estimating just the current platform.

07:59.280 --> 08:05.680
Sometimes we are also interested in estimating the full trajectory or the full path that the platform has taken.

08:05.680 --> 08:09.080
Quite often we are referring here to as just estimating the current location.

08:09.080 --> 08:13.920
So it would be just a three-dimensional or six-dimensional vector.

08:13.920 --> 08:19.800
There's also the word pose being used pretty often. Pose describes here position and heading information.

08:19.800 --> 08:25.640
So the position information would be here, for example, XYZ and then the heading would be roll, pitch and yaw.

08:25.640 --> 08:31.040
So if you hear the word pose, it typically means position and heading information put together.

08:31.040 --> 08:33.520
And that's the quantity that we want to estimate.

08:33.520 --> 08:40.120
So in probabilistic state estimation, we are concerned with estimating the probability distribution about where is the platform,

08:40.120 --> 08:44.360
about our state, given our observation and our control commands.

08:44.360 --> 08:49.960
So describing this in its most simple form, again, use a control command,

08:49.960 --> 08:55.520
Z is the observation and X is the state that I want to estimate.

08:55.520 --> 09:03.760
And often it boils down to estimating this probability distribution, or it could also be just estimating the most likely state is also an option.

09:03.760 --> 09:10.320
But given that we are in the probabilistic framework and we also want to take into account the uncertainty that we have about our pose estimate,

09:10.320 --> 09:19.080
often we're actually looking into estimating really the probability distribution, which could be Gaussian, but could also be a different type of representation.

09:19.080 --> 09:25.960
And as we are in most online problems and navigation problems, concerned with the question, where am I now?

09:25.960 --> 09:29.800
So not where have I been 20 minutes ago, but where am I now?

09:29.800 --> 09:35.640
We are often using recursive state estimation techniques, so filtering techniques in order to perform this task.

09:35.640 --> 09:45.440
And those filtering techniques most of the time are based on the base filter or recursive base filter that we have been discussing here in the past before.

09:45.440 --> 09:57.000
So the belief about where the platform is right now, so the probability distribution of our state at time t is estimated given our observations and our control commands that we have received so far.

09:57.000 --> 10:04.960
And the recursive base filter allows us to put this equation into a recursive form so that the same equation belief at time t minus 1 pops up here again.

10:04.960 --> 10:16.600
So this is the current belief, this is based on the previous belief and then some quantities which only depend on the currently obtained state variables like the current control command and the current observation.

10:16.600 --> 10:23.400
And there are two key components in here, two key distributions in which is the motion model and the observation model.

10:23.400 --> 10:33.160
Again, as a reminder, the observation model describes you how likely it is to get the observation that I actually received given the state I'm currently in.

10:33.160 --> 10:39.960
So given the state, what's the probability of a certain observation or the observation we currently received?

10:39.960 --> 11:03.120
And the motion model describes us how likely is it to end up at a state x t given I know I was at x t minus 1 before and executed a certain motion command like pressing the gas pedal of my car with a certain intensity and turning the car with a given steering angle or putting to the right wheel and the left wheel of my differential drive a certain velocity command.

11:03.160 --> 11:14.400
This describes the motion that the platform will be taking, was likely to be taking, and this motion model describes the probabilistic way on how do we walk from one state to the next state.

11:14.400 --> 11:29.360
And with this information, with the observation and the motion model, we can turn the previous belief into the belief at the current point in time and we are typically recursively estimating our pose, updating our pose estimate, giving such a filtering equation.

11:29.480 --> 11:35.400
Not all localisation systems are based on the idea of a recursive base filter, but several popular ones are.

11:37.440 --> 11:44.000
Another term I would like to distinguish is global localisation with respect to what's called post tracking.

11:44.440 --> 11:48.120
So global localisation means we have no idea where we start.

11:48.120 --> 11:50.120
So we have an unknown initial configuration.

11:50.640 --> 11:54.000
This basically means we can be anywhere and we have no idea where we are.

11:54.480 --> 11:56.400
This is the task of global localisation.

11:56.960 --> 12:03.920
Post tracking means we typically start from a known position or we have a fairly, let's call it, focused belief about where we are.

12:03.920 --> 12:10.040
So belief is a small uncertainty and we just want to estimate where the system is moving, giving that start location.

12:10.080 --> 12:23.440
This is something that's often referred to as post tracking and post tracking is typically the task which is simpler than global localisation because in global localisation we have more ambiguities about what happens in the environment.

12:24.280 --> 12:35.880
So the key differences between those two approaches is that for global localisation approaches, from a technical perspective, I typically need to have a way to represent the large uncertainties really well.

12:36.880 --> 12:43.360
And for post tracking, it's typically sufficient to have a fairly or allow for a fairly compact representation of the uncertainty.

12:43.880 --> 12:52.480
So, for example, if we have a Gaussian distribution, that is something that is often well suited for post tracking, but not necessarily very good for global localisation.

12:53.360 --> 12:56.600
At least in the set up where I have ambiguities in the environment.

12:56.640 --> 13:09.960
So multiple hypotheses where the system could be because I have no idea where the system starts and I need to estimate the location of the platform based on the sense observations that I obtain and I basically need to narrow down the different possibilities of where that platform is.

13:11.200 --> 13:17.160
At the same point in time, global localisation techniques need to have often a way for representing very large uncertainties.

13:17.520 --> 13:26.920
So for a Gaussian distribution, for example, it doesn't really matter how large my uncertainty is as long as it can be well described with a covariance matrix, for example.

13:27.360 --> 13:41.560
Other representations such as sample based representations may suffer in large or large scale global localisation because representing more states about where the system could be in also results in a higher computational or memory complexity.

13:42.080 --> 13:51.560
So depending which problem you're actually addressing, the design of your localisation algorithm may look different, but we also have systems where this is both done together.

13:52.680 --> 14:06.720
So key question that we need to answer ourselves, which are important to answer, which techniques should we use in order to perform global localisation or post tracking is kind of which type of belief do we need to maintain in order to track the position of our system.

14:07.040 --> 14:13.640
Is a Gaussian belief find we need to take into account multimodalities and how much uncertainty can the localisation system handle?

14:13.880 --> 14:21.280
So are we able to deal with very large uncertainties or are we constrained to very kind of local estimates about where the system is?

14:21.560 --> 14:28.680
And this will have an impact on which kind of localisation system you may choose or how you set up your localisation system.

14:29.680 --> 14:38.480
Then we often also distinguish between online and offline localisation and here the question basically is, is all sensor data available beforehand?

14:38.480 --> 14:40.600
Then we typically talk about an offline approach.

14:40.600 --> 14:47.720
So we want to use all the information at hand in order to estimate where the system was at a given point in time or where it was at every point in time.

14:48.800 --> 14:54.200
In contrast to this, online localisation typically means we only have the information available up to a certain point in time.

14:54.200 --> 14:56.080
So up to the current time step, for example.

14:56.520 --> 14:59.320
And we're typically estimating where are we right now.

14:59.440 --> 15:00.280
That's kind of the difference.

15:00.280 --> 15:14.480
So if you think about online navigation, about an autonomous robot navigating through the environment or an autonomous vehicle driving through the world, we are typically interested in online localisation because we want to know where are we right now in order to make our navigation decisions.

15:15.280 --> 15:29.080
If we recorded data and later on post-processed data and maybe want to use the location information to update the map of the environment, then maybe we're interested in offline localisation because we have all the data at hand before we start our computations.

15:29.400 --> 15:38.120
And for us it's more important probably to come up with a good estimate using also kind of the future observations and to estimate where the system was at a certain point in time.

15:38.320 --> 15:44.960
Given that we have all information available beforehand, we will choose an offline localisation approach probably for these types of problems.

15:45.240 --> 15:53.040
But in 95 per cent of the cases or even more, we probably have for an online system because we want to make decisions based on where we are.

15:55.240 --> 16:05.520
Before I dive into the different techniques, I want to distinguish something or a term which is often used, which is referred to as sensor odometry with respect to localisation.

16:06.000 --> 16:09.360
So localisation really means answering the question, where am I?

16:10.040 --> 16:17.080
In contrast to this, sensor odometry means to estimate the relative motion estimates of our platform exploiting sensor information.

16:17.520 --> 16:29.320
So we are interested in estimating local motion updates like local odometry commands, for example, but we don't compute this based on the information that we get from our wheel encoders, for example.

16:29.680 --> 16:37.200
We want to estimate it based on our sensor, for example, based on a laser rangefinder that is observing the environment or based on cameras.

16:37.480 --> 16:45.920
So we often use sensor data and not wheel odometry information in order to compute the relative motion estimates.

16:46.560 --> 16:50.760
And this is typically done by registering or aligning the sensor observation with respect to each other.

16:51.080 --> 16:54.880
Typically, we don't come up with kind of a global pose estimate in here.

16:55.400 --> 16:57.960
So there are some examples that you can have a look to.

16:57.960 --> 17:01.200
One example is visual odometry, for example.

17:01.200 --> 17:06.240
So you're estimating the trajectory of a camera based on the visual information that you're estimating.

17:06.560 --> 17:11.920
So what you see here is a camera stream and you can see those points over here scattered all over the image.

17:11.960 --> 17:13.520
These are feature points extracted.

17:13.520 --> 17:21.560
And based on those feature points, you can try to align one camera image with the next camera image and then can estimate the trajectory of the camera.

17:21.560 --> 17:31.520
So you basically see this in a local view down here, tracking the motion of the camera just based on the consecutive frames that we are receiving.

17:31.600 --> 17:41.520
This is a typical case, visual odometry, where we use our camera, our vision sensor in order to estimate local motion updates, so local odometry, so to say.

17:43.200 --> 17:48.600
And we can use this to estimate where we are, but it's typically not provided in some global frame.

17:49.000 --> 17:50.960
So we talk about visual odometry.

17:51.000 --> 18:00.240
Often visual odometry is combined with inertial sensing, visual inertial odometry, which relates camera positions with each other, often with the help of an IMU.

18:01.240 --> 18:14.000
So if you just have two consecutive frames, so one camera image taken at time t, one taken at time t plus one, we can match features recorded between those images, so distinct points in the world, and then relate them with each other.

18:14.000 --> 18:25.720
For example, using techniques such as the five-point algorithm or the eight-point algorithm, so basically relating one camera image location with respect to the previous one.

18:26.200 --> 18:38.800
And as you may know, this can be determined only up to scale, at least if we have a monocular camera, so we can't estimate how far the cameras are apart, but we can only know in which direction the camera is moved and where the camera is looking to.

18:39.320 --> 18:53.440
And so this is, we can basically determine five degrees of freedom, and then either we use a stereo camera to recover the scale, or we use the IMU in order to provide us with some scale information that we can actually get a 60 degree of freedom transformation.

18:53.440 --> 18:59.560
So delta x, delta y, delta z, and the rotation angles, for example, in order to perform this estimation.

19:00.360 --> 19:13.520
So if you do that, key ingredients of your approach will be some point of way for estimating essential or fundamental matrix of five-point algorithm or eight-point algorithm, depending if you have a calibrated camera or an uncalibrated camera.

19:13.920 --> 19:22.360
Typically, outlier rejection techniques such as RANFEC will be used in this context in order to come up with a robust visual automatry system.

19:22.720 --> 19:32.680
But again, this uses just the camera information to kind of reduce them into a relative motion estimate so we can update our pose.

19:32.960 --> 19:36.000
And of course, also those commands are noisy.

19:36.920 --> 19:42.640
What we can also do is using a LIDAR automatry, so it means we use a LIDAR or laser scanner in order to compute automatry.

19:42.680 --> 19:46.080
That's actually a technique, so LIDAR automatry is more frequently used today.

19:46.560 --> 19:49.200
Before, it was called incremental scan matching, typically.

19:49.440 --> 20:03.520
That means we are registering consecutive scans obtained with the laser range finder in order to estimate how the platform has moved or the recording position or the center, the reference center of my sensor has moved from one time step to the other.

20:03.920 --> 20:05.720
This is done with scan matching technique.

20:06.120 --> 20:13.160
The iterative closest point or variance of the ICP algorithm are here, the standard choice not to register two scans with respect to each other.

20:13.280 --> 20:19.680
And then I know how the platform has moved given this pair of scans and also given data associations.

20:19.680 --> 20:24.920
And given that the LIDAR scanner also provides me with scale information, I don't need an IMU here.

20:25.480 --> 20:27.200
I can exploit it, of course, if I have it.

20:27.440 --> 20:31.160
But otherwise, the system will provide me the scale information.

20:31.160 --> 20:38.120
So I directly get a six degree of freedom transformation out when I'm registering pairs of LIDAR scans.

20:38.640 --> 20:42.760
And this is again something that we refer to as sensor automatry.

20:43.600 --> 20:52.960
It provides relative motion estimates, so updates relative motion commands that are computed based on two or pairs of sensor observations.

20:53.560 --> 20:55.280
It often doesn't really use a global map.

20:55.280 --> 20:57.680
And so the question is, is this really localization?

20:58.200 --> 21:05.560
Often people would say no, that's not really localization because we don't localize ourselves with respect to some coordinate frame.

21:06.360 --> 21:08.760
But of course, it can be helpful to track our pose.

21:08.800 --> 21:14.640
So if we know where we are and we have very good estimate of our relative motion, we can use this to estimate where we are.

21:15.120 --> 21:25.400
So I would see it that sensor automatry is a tool that supports localization because we can use this information to estimate where we are and kind of reduce the uncertainty of our motion.

21:25.720 --> 21:33.760
But it typically doesn't provide me a position estimate with respect to global frame that is provided by a map, for example.

21:35.560 --> 21:41.480
Okay, so now let's dive into the localization problem and see how we can answer the question where we are in the world.

21:41.960 --> 21:50.800
And there are basically four different approaches that are used in order to localize a mobile robot or a platform.

21:51.200 --> 21:55.760
So one is so-called Markov localization or sometimes also use grid localization is used here.

21:56.120 --> 22:06.200
Here we use a discretization of the environment where every kind of this discretization of our world means what the probability that the robot is at that location.

22:06.560 --> 22:15.120
And then similar to an occupancy grid map, but just kind of in more dimensions, we are trying to estimate where the platform is as a kind of nonparametric belief.

22:15.200 --> 22:20.240
There's also sometimes called nonparametric localization or histogram based localization.

22:20.920 --> 22:31.680
Then there's Monte Carlo localization, which is a sampling based technique which is very frequently used in order to estimate the position of a platform, especially supporting multimodal beliefs.

22:32.240 --> 22:35.240
Kalman filter based techniques are also very popular.

22:36.040 --> 22:44.520
Typically, if a Gaussian belief is well suited to represent the location information that one estimate, then the Kalman filter or extended Kalman filter is a good choice.

22:45.040 --> 22:51.800
But we may also run least squares approaches and then we have kind of what's called sliding window least squares.

22:52.040 --> 22:53.800
So least squares is an offline technique.

22:53.840 --> 22:57.640
Those three are online techniques and the sliding window basically sits somewhere in between.

22:57.680 --> 23:02.680
So you can see the sliding window as an interpolation between least squares and the Kalman filter.

23:02.920 --> 23:18.640
And depending on the computational resources you have, you can use this sliding window ideas in order to come up with the least square system, but only estimating the observations that you obtained so far, still being able to execute this in an efficient manner.

23:18.960 --> 23:24.480
And the sliding window based approaches have certain advantages, especially if you want to fuse different sensing modalities.

23:25.480 --> 23:33.320
But common approaches to robot localization are actually Monte Carlo localization, probably the most frequently used one, and Kalman filter based localization.

23:34.080 --> 23:44.920
I just want to illustrate those different techniques here to give you an overview and then you have the possibility to dive deeper into individual aspects such as Monte Carlo localization or Kalman filter based localization.

23:45.600 --> 23:48.680
Okay, let's start with Markov localization or grid localization.

23:49.200 --> 23:54.440
So here the belief about where the platform is in the environment is typically represented with a histogram basically.

23:55.000 --> 23:56.200
So we have here this example.

23:56.200 --> 23:59.640
So we have a one dimensional world just to make it easier to illustrate over here.

24:00.000 --> 24:03.800
We have a robot which can move through the environment and it basically can sense doors.

24:03.800 --> 24:08.320
This is a sensor which says I'm in front of the door and it says I see a door and otherwise it says nothing.

24:09.000 --> 24:15.680
And so then the question is how can we use this information of a robot that navigates through this 1D world and estimate where it is.

24:16.240 --> 24:29.680
And what Markov localization does, it basically uses a histogram which is kind of illustrated over here in order to estimate what's the probability that the robot is within a certain small interval, let's say within a five centimeter region.

24:30.480 --> 24:37.880
And so I'm basically discretizing this environment into these kind of five centimeter cells and then estimate the probability that the robot is in that cell.

24:38.080 --> 24:40.600
So you can see this is a 1D histogram.

24:40.960 --> 24:44.240
If the robot moves in 2D worlds, this would be a two dimensional histogram.

24:44.560 --> 24:50.160
If you also want to estimate its orientation, it would be three dimensional, so X, Y and the orientation theta.

24:50.480 --> 24:56.760
And if you go to six dimensions or a six degree of freedom, that would be kind of a 6D histogram, which gets pretty complex quite quickly.

24:57.360 --> 25:11.480
So for typical 2D localizations, if we want to localize in an indoor environment and we have a wheel platform which kind of drives on the ground, it's not flying through the environment such as a UAV, a 3D histogram or 3D grid representing this belief is typically what you're using.

25:12.480 --> 25:17.280
The great thing about this approach is that it naturally handles multimodal beliefs.

25:17.320 --> 25:19.400
So I can have as many modes as I want.

25:19.680 --> 25:24.520
It's just kind of that I can have a probability value for every cell.

25:24.560 --> 25:28.040
So it's very easy to handle multimodal beliefs.

25:28.080 --> 25:29.480
There's nothing I need to do for that.

25:30.800 --> 25:35.760
However, it has limitations with respect to the accuracy, the localization accuracy that I can obtain.

25:36.080 --> 25:39.800
And it basically tells us how large are those sizes, those cells.

25:39.840 --> 25:49.280
If I have five centimeter cells, it basically means I can only localize my platform up to this cell size of five centimeters or five by five centimeters.

25:49.760 --> 26:03.800
And if you think about especially larger high dimensional space, if you think about a 6D space, X, Y, that, your pitch roll, you can imagine that you actually really don't have much memory available in order to provide a fine grained discretization here.

26:04.200 --> 26:15.320
That's the reason why this approach is to be not used for 3D localization in the 3D world where you would need 6D representation, but for 2D you still sometimes find these approaches.

26:15.880 --> 26:23.480
Another downside is that all cells need to be updated upon every motion command or every observation that is obtained, which gets computationally very costly.

26:23.560 --> 26:28.720
So even if you can store that easily in memory, it gets computationally expensive to perform this operation.

26:29.720 --> 26:33.920
So if you, for example, have the platform, it stands in front of the door.

26:33.960 --> 26:35.800
We have no idea where the system is initially.

26:35.800 --> 26:38.280
So we have basically a global localization approach.

26:38.280 --> 26:42.040
So we have the same uncertainty in all positions that the platform is.

26:42.360 --> 26:44.200
And then the sensor says, I'm in front of a door.

26:44.200 --> 26:44.960
I see a door.

26:45.440 --> 26:49.480
That means the observation model would probably look like this.

26:49.480 --> 26:51.760
So we get peaks in front of these doors.

26:51.760 --> 26:53.320
This is kind of what a door sensor says.

26:53.920 --> 26:59.680
Here the probability is not zero because it could be that the sensor makes a mistake and then we can also be anywhere in the environment.

27:00.760 --> 27:05.600
But if we are in front of a door, it's more likely to provide us with the right information.

27:06.120 --> 27:09.440
As a result of this, our histogram might be updated and may look like this.

27:09.440 --> 27:14.000
So all the positions which lie in front of the door will have an increased probability.

27:14.920 --> 27:20.360
If then in the next step the robot moves through the environment, we basically need to shift this histogram according to the direction of movement.

27:20.600 --> 27:25.320
And we probably also kind of smear out that histogram over here because the motion again is uncertain.

27:25.360 --> 27:28.560
So we increase the uncertainty due to the motion of the platform.

27:29.200 --> 27:35.800
If you then get the next observation, we are in front of the second door, then we again multiply this observation likelihood with our belief.

27:36.080 --> 27:38.520
And then we actually get a belief which looks like this.

27:38.560 --> 27:42.800
So the probability of being here in that position gets larger and larger and larger.

27:42.960 --> 27:49.560
And then we can again move through the environment and then this histogram flattens again a little bit because I'm getting more uncertain about the environment.

27:49.600 --> 27:51.800
Even if I don't receive any further sensor information.

27:52.840 --> 27:56.160
So that's basically the idea of Markov localization or grid localization.

27:56.200 --> 28:03.640
So I'm using a histogram about the states where the system is in and estimate where the robot is based on that histogram.

28:04.320 --> 28:07.320
That was Markov localization also called grid localization.

28:08.440 --> 28:11.400
The second approach I want to dive into is Monte Carlo localization.

28:12.280 --> 28:17.680
So Monte Carlo localization is an alternative technique where the belief is different.

28:17.680 --> 28:23.480
So we're not using histogram, we're using random samples and every sample is basically a state hypothesis.

28:23.840 --> 28:31.080
So it's a guess where the system is with respect to an X, Y, theta or X, Y that your pitch roll state.

28:31.680 --> 28:39.480
So if this is my robot navigating through the environment, you can see these kind of black lines over here and these represent the position of samples.

28:39.480 --> 28:41.200
So the state configuration of a sample.

28:41.840 --> 28:43.520
In this case, it's just one dimensional.

28:43.520 --> 28:46.600
It's just kind of the position where the platform is here.

28:46.680 --> 28:52.960
Located in, but the key idea is that we don't have this discretization, this histogram of the environment.

28:52.960 --> 28:59.280
But we are using random samples in order to represent the belief about where the platform is in the environment.

28:59.600 --> 29:00.960
Then we can do the same thing.

29:00.960 --> 29:05.960
So we can basically uniformly or roughly uniformly initialize our system if we have no idea where the platform is.

29:06.120 --> 29:10.080
If the robot stands in front of the door in the same observation likelihood.

29:10.080 --> 29:15.040
And so basically seeing that all those samples which are here beside those beliefs, they get amplified.

29:15.520 --> 29:16.760
It means they get more important.

29:16.920 --> 29:21.360
We will dive into that in more details how that works in the next lectures.

29:21.600 --> 29:24.880
But for now, we just say, okay, those samples are better than the other samples.

29:24.880 --> 29:26.960
It's more likely that we're here at that space.

29:27.360 --> 29:35.760
And if the platform then moved through the environment and can see actually samples getting more denser over here, if I get new observation, this holds here as well.

29:35.760 --> 29:40.240
So the robot moved through the environment and we are getting kind of clusters of those beliefs.

29:40.240 --> 29:44.880
And the more of those samples we have in a certain region, that means the more likely that region actually is.

29:45.880 --> 29:55.080
So this idea of Monte Carlo localization in order to be able to represent multimodal beliefs, but not using a fixed discretization of the environment.

29:55.080 --> 30:00.800
And this is done through a sample-based representation, which is also one of the popular localization techniques out there.

30:01.360 --> 30:10.160
So especially if you look into indoor localization techniques where no GPS or other things are available, then Monte Carlo localization is probably the gold standard today.

30:11.000 --> 30:13.000
Other alternatives are

30:13.400 --> 30:20.560
Kalman filter-based localization. So here we typically have Gaussian beliefs in measurements and also observation models.

30:20.560 --> 30:23.280
So everything is assumed to be Gaussian and then

30:24.000 --> 30:28.680
we can estimate where the platform is by updating Gaussian distributions using a Kalman filter.

30:29.160 --> 30:33.880
So quite often this is used in the context when I want to localize landmarks because the

30:34.040 --> 30:39.840
estimation of a landmark location can be quite well described with a Gaussian distribution, at least if I'm free of outliers.

30:40.320 --> 30:45.680
And then I can have a kind of a trajectory of a robot, how a robot is driving through the environment shown over here,

30:46.040 --> 30:52.320
certain observation that it gets and then I can provide a Gaussian estimate about where the platform is in the environment.

30:52.560 --> 30:56.360
And here we are basically predicting the state of the system using a Gaussian.

30:56.360 --> 31:03.000
We are performing a correction using our observations and recursively update our belief using this Gaussian distributions.

31:03.000 --> 31:09.640
It's also a commonly used technique, actually a lot of localization techniques started as Kalman filter-based localization techniques.

31:09.880 --> 31:16.120
And that works well as long as the assumption that my beliefs are Gaussian is not a too strong

31:16.680 --> 31:20.560
violation of what happens actually in reality, especially with respect to the current belief.

31:20.800 --> 31:25.640
So often, especially for global localization, this is not working that great,

31:26.200 --> 31:29.320
especially if you have multi-modalities in the environment.

31:29.800 --> 31:35.000
Okay, so the next technique I want to look into is least squares or least squares approach to localization.

31:35.120 --> 31:38.880
This is typically an offline approach, not an online approach anymore,

31:38.880 --> 31:45.880
where I assume to have all the sensor information available beforehand and then use all the sensor information in order to

31:46.320 --> 31:51.760
compute the typical trajectory, so all pulses of the platform or maybe only just one position.

31:51.760 --> 31:58.600
And this uses a standard least squares approach and the standard least squares error minimization approach that you

31:59.120 --> 32:01.880
know already, where we want to estimate the

32:02.680 --> 32:07.640
discrepancy between what the observation tells us and what the motion tells us in a global setup,

32:07.640 --> 32:10.080
so having all observation at hand beforehand.

32:10.480 --> 32:16.320
The least squares approach typically also takes Gaussian beliefs into account, but as it's doing real linearizations at every point in time,

32:16.320 --> 32:20.000
it works somewhat better than the extended Kalman filter.

32:20.000 --> 32:25.000
But if I'm very far away from the idea of a Gaussian distribution, it also may not work very well.

32:26.000 --> 32:31.400
Quite often, least squares approaches are used kind of as reference solutions in order to estimate other

32:32.240 --> 32:34.240
localization systems by kind of

32:34.780 --> 32:40.840
recording all the data, making sure we don't have any outliers, providing a proper estimate, taking all observations into account,

32:41.160 --> 32:47.200
computing a statistically optimal solution under the assumptions, and then using this as a reference position and compare online

32:47.880 --> 32:54.480
localization systems with respect to this offline approach where all the information is available beforehand.

32:55.000 --> 33:01.520
There are different ways how we can represent this. One way which became more and more popular over the recent years are factor graphs.

33:01.520 --> 33:08.920
This is a graphical representation of the least squares problem where we have states, in this case, posters that we want to estimate, so the position

33:09.560 --> 33:16.740
at time 0, time 1, time 2, time 3, then have landmark observations here and XL for the landmark location.

33:17.400 --> 33:22.880
We have observations. These are represented by those factors, so seeing a landmark from a different positions.

33:22.880 --> 33:27.080
We have odometry factors in between saying how did the system evolve over time?

33:27.200 --> 33:30.640
We may have GNSS information or GPS information available.

33:30.680 --> 33:38.700
We can build up this type of factor graphs where I basically want to compute configurations for those nodes so that the factors or the

33:39.840 --> 33:45.320
the errors introduced by those factors actually gets minimized. It turns into a least squares problem,

33:45.320 --> 33:50.660
but these factor graphs are quite attractive visual representation of the underlying problem. And

33:51.100 --> 33:57.260
Frank Dillard is, for example, one of those persons who made factor graphs popular in robotics used for the SLAM problem for localization and

33:57.460 --> 34:05.620
other types of problems. And it's an attractive formulation because it allows to visually describe a least squares problem in a fairly elegant way.

34:05.620 --> 34:12.980
And you can transform this one basically directly into a matrix of a normal system where you can store an information matrix

34:12.980 --> 34:14.980
and where you can see then directly

34:15.300 --> 34:19.740
the contributions of those factors to certain elements in your matrix.

34:19.980 --> 34:24.460
And last but not least, we have these sliding window based approaches

34:25.420 --> 34:31.980
which basically use the least squares approach, but basically doesn't take the whole history into account. Because the more data I

34:33.340 --> 34:39.700
record, the more data I need to take into account in my least squares problem. And what the sliding window least squares approach does, it

34:39.700 --> 34:41.700
basically takes only the most recent N

34:42.200 --> 34:44.200
observations into account. Let's say the most recent

34:44.560 --> 34:51.040
20 observations and then tries to approximate everything which happened before with one single variable.

34:51.040 --> 34:53.040
And so while moving through the environment,

34:53.040 --> 34:58.400
I'm basically getting a new observation in and throwing out a new an old one and trying to estimate a good belief about where the

34:58.400 --> 34:59.800
system is in the environment.

34:59.800 --> 35:07.160
So the key idea is to be better than a Kalman filter and this requires to do a proper modernization of the information that I

35:07.160 --> 35:14.120
take out of my least squares problem, but still not be computationally as expensive as a least squares problem.

35:14.320 --> 35:19.480
And this is a technique which you find today quite often, especially for outdoor localization.

35:19.480 --> 35:22.740
So if you think about an autonomous cars driving through the environment,

35:22.740 --> 35:29.040
they typically use some type of least squares or sliding window least squares approach in order to estimate their position.

35:29.260 --> 35:35.360
Taking different sensors into account such as GSNS information, visual odometry, wheel odometry, light odometry, radar information

35:35.360 --> 35:41.860
maybe and other sources in order to estimate that they fuse this information with a factor graph

35:41.900 --> 35:46.500
which basically looks like this and chops of nodes which are old one and adds new nodes over here

35:46.740 --> 35:49.460
taking a certain small history into account.

35:49.460 --> 35:56.140
And we can see the sliding window approaches are sitting somewhere between the least squares approaches and the Kalman filter based approaches.

35:56.780 --> 36:02.540
And basically depending on where I am on that line, I have a very short sliding window and the sliding window of size one

36:02.540 --> 36:06.900
I basically end up having a Kalman filter or if I have an infinitively large sliding window approach

36:06.900 --> 36:08.820
then I'm actually performing a least squares approach.

36:08.860 --> 36:15.660
You can use the sliding window, the size of your sliding window basically to control how many computational resources you have at every time step

36:15.660 --> 36:18.300
available in order to solve your localization problem.

36:18.300 --> 36:24.380
And in this way turn this offline least squares approach kind of in an online approach taking only the most recent and

36:24.920 --> 36:26.540
observations in order to

36:26.540 --> 36:28.540
do your computations.

36:28.660 --> 36:33.380
Often in this case approximations are also made with terms of how to throw away

36:33.380 --> 36:36.740
so to say the old information that I take out of this graph structure.

36:36.740 --> 36:43.060
So the marginalization because exact marginalization can be computationally expensive depending on how your graph looks like and therefore

36:43.460 --> 36:50.780
approximation are often made on the way. I brought you a small example. And so this is a localization system of an

36:51.540 --> 36:54.400
autonomous car driving through the environment. It's a system

36:55.100 --> 36:58.820
developed by one of my PhD students Daniel Wilbas.

36:58.820 --> 37:04.820
You see a vehicle driving through the environment and you can see build up basically a graph which is built up here

37:04.820 --> 37:10.860
so you see kind of certain edges which are here illustrated as observations so they could observe buildings, you could observe poles,

37:11.380 --> 37:13.380
corners which provides additional information

37:13.860 --> 37:17.980
to the system and then the vehicle can drive through the environment.

37:17.980 --> 37:24.620
You can also observe new features, use different types of objects in the environment such as poles, such as road markings.

37:24.620 --> 37:30.940
It observes those road markings and basically builds up this graph and this graph that you can see over here is the

37:31.100 --> 37:35.260
factor graph that I was talking about and you can also see here that this is not an infinitely long line.

37:35.260 --> 37:40.900
It's basically chopped off over here. So this may be a trajectory of whatever 30-50 meters

37:40.900 --> 37:47.380
maybe. It's basically throw away all the old information which sits in here and basically takes information away at the back and adds new

37:47.380 --> 37:49.980
information at the top trying to estimate a

37:50.220 --> 37:56.500
good estimate about where the vehicles in the environment without any jumps taking into account multiple sensing modalities.

37:56.620 --> 37:58.720
Just as one example how such

37:59.480 --> 38:07.480
localization system can be used and actually an up-to-date localization system used in prototype autonomous vehicles that are built in the industry today.

38:08.080 --> 38:14.160
With this I'm coming to an end of the overview lecture that I've given here about robot or vehicle localization and

38:14.920 --> 38:22.920
in subsequent lectures we may dive deeper into different aspects or different techniques for localization such as Kalman filter-based localization,

38:23.120 --> 38:26.380
Monte Carlo localization or similar. So in some

38:27.380 --> 38:32.300
localization means we want to estimate the position and the heading of a mobile system in the environment.

38:32.820 --> 38:34.820
Central building blocks that we

38:34.820 --> 38:40.540
that we use for this are our observation model, our sensor model and the map of the environment and

38:41.020 --> 38:47.960
localization itself is a key element for other tasks that are used by mobile robots such as navigating, task planning,

38:49.120 --> 38:50.700
collision avoidance,

38:50.700 --> 38:53.860
picking tasks, delivery tasks. For all those tasks you need to know where you are.

38:54.260 --> 38:58.020
So whenever you move efficiently through the environment, you should know where you are.

38:58.020 --> 39:02.300
Otherwise, you can't navigate well and localization provides this key building block.

39:03.020 --> 39:08.820
I guess I've shown that there are different variants how localization can be run, can be online, can be offline,

39:09.220 --> 39:12.940
with a map, without a map, a global localization, post tracking.

39:12.940 --> 39:19.820
So there's a large set of different variants and actually hundreds of papers on localization that are out there and are still being published.

39:20.340 --> 39:24.900
If you look to the underlying estimation techniques, we basically find four types of techniques.

39:24.940 --> 39:29.040
This is the grid-based or Markov localization. This is a Kalman filter.

39:29.040 --> 39:35.240
This is Monte Carlo localization and least squares and again a variant of the least squares, a sliding window least squares approach.

39:35.740 --> 39:40.380
These are techniques that are often used in modern and up-to-date localization systems.

39:40.980 --> 39:43.580
Several of these approaches basically realize a base filter,

39:43.580 --> 39:49.780
but the least squares approach doesn't belong to this category because we don't have a recursive belief where we only integrate the most recent

39:49.940 --> 39:55.320
observation. I'm either taking all observations into account or a certain window of observation.

39:55.320 --> 39:56.680
So this is not a base filter,

39:56.680 --> 40:02.840
but the Kalman filter, Monte Carlo localization as well as Markov localization are base filter-based approaches.

40:03.260 --> 40:05.500
With this, I say thank you very much for your attention.

40:05.500 --> 40:10.060
And again, there's a short five-minute video, kind of a summary of this lecture available as well.

40:10.460 --> 40:18.000
So thank you very much for your attention and I hope this gave you a good introduction on what localization is before diving deeper into studying the individual techniques.

40:18.000 --> 40:20.160
Thank you very much for your attention.

